WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.405
Tokenizer compatibility matters because the model was trained to interpret text through a

00:00:04.405 --> 00:00:06.000
specific tokenization process.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:10.000
Special tokens may mark the beginning of text, user messages, assistant messages,

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:12.000
system prompts, or stop conditions.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.125
If those tokens are missing or misused, the model may still generate text,

00:00:15.125 --> 00:00:18.000
but it may not follow instructions in the way the user expects.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.000
Chat templates define how conversational prompts are formatted for a model.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Two models may both support chat,

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.000
but one may expect a system message in a particular structure while another expects

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.000
different role markers or separators.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.035
A web UI or runtime may apply the template automatically,

00:00:32.035 --> 00:00:34.000
but users should still understand the concept.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.000
Prompt formatting is part of model compatibility, not just interface style.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:42.000
Incorrect formatting can produce weak, oddly structured, or less aligned output.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.000
The model may ignore a system instruction, continue a user message instead of answering,

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:47.990
include unnecessary role labels, or stop too early.

00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:52.000
These issues can look like model quality problems when the real cause is configuration.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.020
Before blaming a model, verify that the runtime is using the right tokenizer,

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:59.000
chat template, stop tokens, and context settings.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:02.860
Metadata helps runtimes load and use models correctly by carrying facts that would

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:04.990
otherwise need to be supplied manually.

00:01:04.990 --> 00:01:08.990
It can identify architecture, tokenizer configuration, quantization information,

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:10.990
and sometimes prompt-related settings.

00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:13.000
Metadata does not remove the need for testing,

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:16.835
but it reduces guesswork and makes local inference workflows more portable across

00:01:16.835 --> 00:01:18.000
compatible tools.
